Independent native relocation oracle
====================================

The initial oracle checks arm64 Mach-O captures from the native runner. Build
that runner as described in ../runner/README. Use LLVM 17 or later; the tools
are mandatory and a missing tool fails the check.

Check a capture and its committed semantic reference:

    python3 liblink/tests/oracle/reloc_check.py \
      --object liblink/tests/fixtures/generated/macho-arm64/ARM64_RELOC_UNSIGNED.o \
      --capture capture.json --readobj "$LLVM_READOBJ" \
      --golden liblink/tests/golden/arm64/ARM64_RELOC_UNSIGNED.json \
      --evidence-dir check-results

Run the native suite with a built runner:

    LIBLINK_TEST_RUNNER=/absolute/path/to/liblink-runner \
      LLVM_READOBJ=/absolute/path/to/llvm-readobj \
      LLVM_MC=/absolute/path/to/llvm-mc \
      python3 -m unittest discover -s liblink/tests/oracle -p 'test_oracle.py' -v

The suite checks all supported native fixtures, the expected unsupported
POINTER_TO_GOT case, and mutations that corrupt fields, claimed symbols,
addends, GOT slots, stub code, metadata, or bytes outside relocation fields.
An additional page-carry case checks the oracle against llvm-mc encodings.

macho_metadata.py parses LLVM's expanded Mach-O sections, symbols, and
relocations, then binds them to the original file. macho_arm64.py calculates
S from those symbols and independently supplied host addresses, P from layout,
and A from original field bytes and ADDEND records. It checks every relocated
field and every byte of the resulting sections. GOT and stub addresses are
layout inputs; their contents and targets must also pass independent checks.
Backend-observed S, P, A, and relocated bytes are never expected values.

A golden stores the checked numeric fields, the sampled layout, and a semantic
signature. golden_reference.py replays the saved observations through the
oracle before comparing the current signature. The signature uses symbol
names and section/stub offsets, allowing different ASLR addresses. Each fresh
capture must first pass the numerical checks. Initial numeric samples remain
reviewable in the reference files. --write-golden creates a new file only;
replacement is explicit outside this command and needs review.

Sources for the arithmetic are Apple's Mach-O arm64 relocation definitions
and the Arm AArch64 relocation field definitions:

https://github.com/apple-oss-distributions/xnu/blob/main/EXTERNAL_HEADERS/mach-o/arm64/reloc.h
https://github.com/ARM-software/abi-aa/blob/main/aaelf64/aaelf64.rst

The Apple SDK's mach-o/arm64/reloc.h provides further object-file examples.
No backend implementation is imported. Other formats, local relocation
forms, and post-NO_EXEC coverage are later extensions; unsupported oracle
inputs fail explicitly.

Unsupported relocation captures use the compatibility linker's status-returning
failure path. Their expected exit and complete diagnostic remain strict; the
literal `{object}` in a diagnostic expands only to that fixture's exact input
path. This keeps the expected text portable across checkouts. The former abort
expectation predates the approved error-model conversion. The unchanged
WP2.5 base and format-dispatch candidate both return exit 5 for POINTER_TO_GOT.
Numeric relocation goldens and the independent arithmetic checks are unchanged.

ELF x86_64 address checks
-------------------------

elf_x86_64_check.py reads original ELF64 little-endian section, symbol and RELA
records using Python's struct module. It computes S from defined symbols or
separately recorded resolver inputs, P from section layout, and A from the
original signed RELA addend. The formulas follow AMD64 psABI table4.9:

https://gitlab.com/x86-psABIs/x86-64-ABI/-/jobs/artifacts/master/raw/x86-64-ABI/abi.pdf?job=build

All nine supported ordinary fixtures are checked, including all three GOTPCREL
forms. The oracle verifies whole resulting sections and extras, including
untouched bytes and the native x64 GOT/jump-island payload. It does not import
the linker or derive an expected S from a relocated field. Saved references
are replayed numerically before comparing ASLR-independent semantic signatures.
The initial goldens were captured with the unchanged WP2.7 linker at102bd9.

    python3 liblink/tests/oracle/elf_x86_64_check.py \
      --object liblink/tests/fixtures/generated/elf-x86_64/x86_64-linux-gnu/R_X86_64_64.o \
      --capture capture.json --golden liblink/tests/golden/elf-x86_64/R_X86_64_64.json

    python3 -m unittest discover -s liblink/tests/oracle -p test_elf_x86_64.py -v

Set LIBLINK_ELF_CAPTURE to the absolute elf_capture executable to check fresh
normal and NO_EXEC captures, including a forced PLT32 island with an
out-of-range resolver input. Each NO_EXEC fixture runs twice against its
reference in ../golden/elf-x86_64-noexec. Those references add exact synthetic
execution addresses and SHA-256 hashes for relocated sections, extras and the
global GOT. The numerical check derives global GOT payloads from original
symbols/resolver inputs, and verifies work/exec separation and fixed x64 page
alignment. Heap addresses are sampled layout inputs, not deterministic output.

Corruption controls alter capture JSON, not object-file inputs. They reject
wrong GOT payloads, work/exec aliases, misaligned synthetic addresses and
altered saved image hashes as well as native relocation errors. TLS and other
architectures remain outside this oracle's supported inputs. See ../runner/README
for capture scope and build flags. These local checks do not establish two-host
golden stability or foreign execution qualification.

ELF AArch64 normal-address baselines
-----------------------------------

elf_aarch64_check.py uses the shared bounded elf64_metadata.py parser for
original little-endian ELF64 records. Relocation formulas follow Arm AAELF64
2025Q4 sections5.7.5–5.7.7:

https://github.com/ARM-software/abi-aa/blob/main/aaelf64/aaelf64.rst

The oracle checks every nonempty original section, all GOT payloads and all
reserved stub bytes. It derives S from original symbols and explicit resolver
inputs, P from layout, and A from original RELA records. GOT slot identity
uses the original table/index even when two symbols have equal values. Far
branches must reach the independently encoded veneer; stub target, reuse,
order and untouched capacity remain checked. The veneer encoder also matches
llvm-mc for four full-width addresses.

    LIBLINK_ELF_AARCH64_CAPTURE=/absolute/path/to/aarch64-target-elf-capture \
      LLVM_MC=/absolute/path/to/llvm-mc \
      python3 -m unittest discover -s liblink/tests/oracle -p test_elf_aarch64.py -v

Twenty saved layouts cover27 fields from the thirteen ordinary fixtures plus
negative PREL32/ADRP, near/far branch reuse and equal-valued GOT aliases.
Mutation controls reject incomplete captures, wrong fields/GOT/veneer bytes,
changed stub identity/order, altered resolver inputs, work/exec mismatch and
corrupt saved numeric references. Native ASLR changes layout but must preserve
the semantic signature after numerical verification. The initial captures use
the unchanged542259edba9 object backend on an x64 host with an AArch64 target.

Scope is one symbol table, ordinary supported RELA forms, zero GOT addends,
and zero branch addends when a veneer is needed. Other inputs fail explicitly.
The NO_EXEC references additionally record deterministic execution addresses,
section and veneer hashes, and global GOT contents. Work and execution layouts
must be separate. Each live reference runs twice when the AArch64 capture is
available. The classifier reads one instruction word so short sections remain
within bounds. Two-host and AArch64 execution qualification remain open.

ELF RISC-V normal-mode checks
----------------------------

elf_riscv64_check.py parses the original ELF records and independently checks
23 reference fixtures, including branch encodings, paired PC-relative HI/LO
relocations, GOT entries, call veneers, unchanged bytes and the backend's
existing 32-bit operand narrowing. The baseline does not assert NO_EXEC or
native RISC-V execution. To replay saved captures and corruption controls:

    python3 -m unittest discover -s liblink/tests/oracle -p test_elf_riscv64.py -v

Set LIBLINK_ELF_RISCV64_CAPTURE to a compatible capture executable to repeat the
checks against live data-only captures. The initial mixed-target Darwin build
uses host-target Mach-O units and does not qualify native RISC-V execution.
The RISC-V ELF psABI specifies relocation and instruction field encodings:
https://github.com/riscv-non-isa/riscv-elf-psabi-doc/blob/master/riscv-elf.adoc
